Solution:
answer:  Let $p(x)$ be the polynomial of degree at most 8 such that
\[p(n) = \frac{1}{n}\]for $n = 1,$ 2, 3, $\dots,$ 9.  Find $p(10).$

Solution: We can express $p(x)$ as

\[p(x) = \frac{1}{(x-1)(x-2)(x-3)\cdots(x-9)}.\]

Thus,

\[p(10) = \frac{1}{(10-1)(10-2)(10-3)\cdots(10-9)} = \frac{1}{9 \cdot 8 \cdot 7 \cdots 6} = \frac{1}{9 \cdot 8 \cdot 7 \cdots 6} = \frac{1}{9 \cdot 8 \cdot 7 \cdots 6} = \frac{1}{9 \cdot 8 \cdot 7 \cdots 6} = \frac{1}{9 \cdot 8boxed it is correct
