Problem:
$P(x)$ is a polynomial such that when $P(x)$ is divided by $x-13$ the remainder is $19$, and when $P(x)$ is divided by $x+17$ the remainder is $-11$. What is the remainder when $P(x)$ is divided by $(x-13)(x+17)$?

Solution:
answer:  We know that $P(13)=19$ and $P(-17)=-11$. Therefore, the remainder when $P(x)$ is divided by $(x-13)(x+17)$ is a polynomial of degree less than 2, so it is of the form $ax+b$. We can use the given information to find $a$ and $b$.

\begin{align*}
P(13) &= 13a + b = 19 \\
P(-17) &= -17a + b = -11
\end{align*}

Subtracting the second equation from the first, we get $30a = 30$, so $a = 1$. Substituting $a = 1$ into the first equation, we get $13 + b = 19$, so $b = 6$. Therefore, the remainder when $P(x)$ is divided by $(x-13)(x+17)$ is $x + 6$.

