Problem:
Let $f(x) = \frac{2x+9}{x-7}.$ Find the product of all solutions to the equation $f(f(x)) = x.$

Solution:
answer:  We have that $f(f(x)) = f\left(\frac{2x+9}{x-7}\right) = \frac{2\left(\frac{2x+9}{x-7}\right) + 9}{\frac{2x+9}{x-7} - 7} = \frac{\frac{4x+18 + 9(x-7)}{x-7}}{\frac{2x+9 - 7(x-7)}{x-7}} = \frac{2x+9}{x-7}.$ Therefore, the equation $f(f(x)) = x$ becomes $\frac{2x+9}{x-7} = x.$ Solving this equation, we get $x = 9$ or $x = -1.$ The product of these solutions is $9 \cdot (-1) = \boxed{-9}.$
