Problem:
Trey receives a $5\%$ commission on every sale he makes. On the sale of a $\$60$ coat (before any discounts), how many more cents will he receive if his commission is based on the original price of the coat rather than the price of the coat after a $20\%$ discount?

Solution:
answer:  First, calculate the price of the coat after a $20\%$ discount:
\[ \text{Discounted price} = 60 \times 0.80 = 48 \]

Next, calculate the commission based on the original price:
\[ \text{Commission on original price} = 60 \times 0.05 = 3 \]

Then, calculate the commission based on the discounted price:
\[ \text{Commission on discounted price} = 48 \times 0.05 = 2.4 \]

Now, find the difference in commission:
\[ \text{Difference in commission} = 3 - 2.4 = 0.6 \]

Convert the difference from dollars to cents:
\[ \text{Difference in cents} = 0.6 \times 100 = 60 \]

