Problem:
A girls' track team must run 3 miles on the first day of practice and 6 miles every day after that. The boys' team must run 5 miles every day of practice. The coach will order new javelins at the end of the day that each girl's total mileage surpasses each boy's. How many total miles will each girl have run by the time the coach orders the new equipment?

Solution:
answer:  Let $n$ be the number of days of practice. The girls' team runs 3 miles on the first day and 6 miles every day after that, so their total mileage is $3 + 6(n-1) = 6n - 3$. The boys' team runs 5 miles every day, so their total mileage is $5n$. We want to find the smallest $n$ such that $6n - 3 > 5n$. Solving this inequality, we get $n > 3$. Therefore, the smallest $n$ that satisfies the inequality is $n = 4$. The girls' team will have run $6(4) - 3 = 21$ miles by the time the coach orders the new equipment. The boys' team will have run $5(4) = 20$ miles by the time the coach orders the new equipment. The girls' team will have run $\boxed{21}$ miles.
