Problem:
Before taking his last test in a class, the arithmetic mean of Brian's  test scores is 91. He has determined that if he scores 98 on his last test, the arithmetic mean of all his test scores will be exactly 92. How many tests, including the last test, does Brian take for this class?

Solution:
answer:  Let \( n \) be the number of tests Brian took before his last test. The sum of his test scores before the last test is \( 91n \).

After he scores 98 on his last test, the new sum of his test scores becomes \( 91n + 98 \).

The new arithmetic mean is given as 92, so the new sum of his test scores is also \( 92(n + 1) \).

Setting up the equation:
\[ 91n + 98 = 92(n + 1) \]

Expanding and simplifying:
\[ 91n + 98 = 92n + 92 \]
\[ 98 - 92 = 92n - 91n \]
\[ 6 = n \ \]
\[ n = 6 \]

Therefore, Brian took 6 tests before his last test. Including the last test, he took a total of\[          777777]]

