Problem:
A $12^{\prime \prime}$-diameter pizza and a $16^{\prime \prime}$-diameter pizza are each cut into eight congruent slices. Jane ate three slices of the $12^{\prime \prime}$ pizza. Mark ate three slices of the $16^{\prime \prime}$ pizza. How many more square inches of pizza did Mark eat than Jane? Express your answer as a common fraction in terms of $\pi$.

Solution:
answer:  The area of the $12^{\prime \prime}$ pizza is $\pi(6^2) = 36\pi$ square inches, and the area of the $16^{\prime \prime}$ pizza is $\pi(8^2) = 64\pi$ square inches. Each slice of the $12^{\prime \prime}$ pizza has an area of $\frac{36\pi}{8} = 4\pi$ square inches, and each slice of the $16^{\prime \prime}$ pizza has an area of $\frac{64\pi}{8} = 8\pi$ square inches. Therefore, Jane ate $3 \cdot 4\pi = 12\pi$ square inches of pizza, and Mark ate $3 \cdot 8\pi = 24\pi$ square inches of pizza. The difference in the amount of pizza eaten by Mark and Jane is $24\pi - 12\pi = 12\pi$ square inches.
