Problem:
The five tallest buildings in Los Angeles in 1985 had a mean height of 733 feet. The tallest of the five buildings has a height of 858 feet, the shortest of the five 625 feet. If a new building were constructed with a height of 885 feet, by how many feet would it increase the mean height of the five tallest buildings of the city?

Solution:
answer:  The total height of the five tallest buildings in Los Angeles in 1985 is $733 \times 5 = 3665$ feet. The height of the tallest and shortest buildings is $858 + 625 = 1483$ feet. Therefore, the height of the remaining three buildings is $3665 - 1483 = 2282$ feet. If a new building with a height of 885 feet is added, the total height of the five buildings will be $3665 + 885 = 4550$ feet. The new mean height will be $4550 \div 5 = 910$ feet. The increase in the mean height is $910 - 733 = 77$ feet. Therefore, the new building would increase the mean height of the five tallest buildings of the city by $\boxed{77}$ feet.
