answer:  To determine how many squares contain the black center square, we need to identify the position of the black center square within the \(5 \times 5\) grid. The black center square is located at coordinates \((2, 2)\) and has a side length of \(1 \times 1\).

The grid is \(5 \times 5\), so there are \(5 \times 5 = 25\) squares in total. The black center square is a \(1 \times 1\) square, so it occupies exactly one square in the grid.

Therefore, the number of squares that contain the black center square is:
{1}\).{
