Problem:
Bob placed three regular pentagons together at a vertex, thinking he might be able to form a tessellation. However, they left a gap. What is the number of degrees in the measure indicated? [asy]size(100);
path A = dir(0) -- dir(72) -- dir (144) -- dir(216) -- dir (288) -- cycle;
path B = reflect ( dir(72), dir(144)) * A;
path C = reflect( dir(72), dir(0))* A;
draw(rotate(18) *A);
draw(rotate(18) *B);
draw(rotate(18) *C);
label("?", (0,1.5), N);
[/asy]

Solution:
answer:  To determine the measure of one of the interior angles of a regular pentagon, we use the formula for the sum of the interior angles of a polygon:

\[
\text{Sum of interior angles} = (n-2) \times 180^\circ
\]

For a pentagon (\( n = 5 \)):

\[
\text{Sum of interior angles} = (5-2) \times 180^\circ = 3 \times 180^\circ = 540^\circ
\]

Since a pentagon has five equal interior angles:

\[
\text{Each interior angle} = \frac{540^\circ}{5} = 108^\circ
\]

When three regular pentagons meet at a vertex, the sum of the interior angles at that vertex is:

\[
3 \times 108^\circ = 324^\circ
\]

Thus, the measure of the in indicated is:




\boxed{324circcirc

 I hope it is correct.
