Six horses eat 12 bales of hay in 12 hours. At the same rate, how many hours will 36 bales of hay last 12 horses?

Solution:
answer:  First, let's determine the rate at which the horses eat hay. Six horses eat 12 bales in 12 hours, so the rate is:

\[
\frac{12 \text{ bales}}{12 \text{ hours}} = 1 \text{ bale per hour}
\]

This means that each horse eats 1 bale of hay per hour. Therefore, 12 horses will eat:

\[
12 \text{ horses} \times 1 \text{ bale per hour} = 12 \text{ bales per hour}
\]

Next, we need to find out how many hours 36 bales of hay will last for 12 horses. Since 12 horses eat 12 bales per hour, 36 bales will last for:

\[
\frac{36 \text{ bales}}{12 \text{ bales per hour}} = 3 \text{ hours}
\]

Therefore, 36 bales of hay will last 12 horses for 3 hours.

