Problem:
One computer in a lab is programmed to back up data at the turn of the minute every five minutes. Another computer is programmed to back up data at the turn of the minute every two minutes. Find the number of times in twenty-four hours that the two computers back up data at the same time.

(Assume that the computers do not back up at the start of the 24-hour period.)

Solution:
answer:  To find the number of times the two computers back up data at the same time in twenty-four hours, we need to determine the least common multiple (LCM) of their backup intervals.

The first computer backs up every 5 minutes, and the second computer backs up every 2 minutes.

The LCM of 5 and 2 is 10. Therefore, the two computers will back up at the same time every 10 minutes.

Next, we need to determine how many 10-minute intervals are in twenty-four hours.

24 hours = 24 * 60 = 1440 minutes

Number of 10-minute intervals in 1440 minutes = 1440 / 10 = 144

Therefore, the two computers back up data at the same time 144 times in twenty-four hours.

