Problem:
Let $\mathbf{a} = \begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ \sqrt{\pi} \\ -14 \end{pmatrix}$ and $\mathbf{b} = \begin{pmatrix} -1 \\ 3 \\ 10 \end{pmatrix}.$  Compute $\mathbf{a} \cdot (\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b}).$

Solution:
answer:  We have that

$$\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b} = \begin{vmatrix} \mathbf{i} & \mathbf{j} & \mathbf{k} \\ 4 & \sqrt{\pi} & -14 \\ -1 & 3 & 10 \end{vmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 10\sqrt{\pi} \\ -30 \\ -56 \end{pmatrix}.$$

Then,

$$\mathbf{a} \cdot (\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b}) = 4(10\sqrt{\pi}) + (\sqrt{\pi})(-30) + (-14)(-56) = 40\sqrt{\pi} - 30\pi + 784 = \boxed{784}.$$

