Problem:
Let $\mathbf{M} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & -5 \\ -2 & 4 \end{pmatrix}.$  There exist scalars $p$ and $q$ such that
\[\mathbf{M}^2 = p \mathbf{M} + q \mathbf{I}.\]Enter the ordered pair $(p,q).$

Solution:
answer:  We have that
\[\mathbf{M}^2 = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & -5 \\ -2 & 4 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} 0 & -5 \\ -2 & 4 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} -20 & -10 \\ -8 & 16 \end{pmatrix}.\]Thus, we want
\[\begin{pmatrix} -20 & -10 \\ -8 & 16 \end{pmatrix} = p \begin{pmatrix} 0 & -5 \\ -2 & 4 \end{pmatrix} + q \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix}.\]Comparing entries, we get
\[p = -\frac{5}{2} \quad \text{and} \quad q = \frac{1}{2}.\]Therefore, the ordered pair $(p,q)$ is $\boxed{\left( -\frac{5}{2}, \frac{1}{2} \right)}.$
